{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Tqj8N137QQw8yWYt7KUP1s","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I've now read the full PDF (13 pages) and the review context YAML. Let me now dispatch two reviewers in parallel as specified by the dream-review-full protocol:\n\n- R1: Methods + Experiments\n- R2: Novelty + Related Work\n\nLet me launch both agents in parallel, then conduct the roundtable process.\n\nLet me summarize what I've read from the paper:\n\n**Title**: \"Is Schema Complexity One-Dimensional? A Controlled Empirical Study of Type Count, Confusability, and Their Interaction in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction\"\n\n**Core claims**:\n\n\nThe paper demonstrates that pairwise confusability effectively predicts type confusion across different models and embeddings, while count and confusability degrade performance through separate mechanisms. They also identify that aggregating per-type confusability introduces a centrality confound, and that confusability-minimizing decomposition doesn't improve performance.\n\n**Setup**: They evaluated 3 IE tasks (Few-NERD NER, MAVEN ED, DocRED RE) across 2 LLMs (Qwen3-32B, LLaMA-3.3-70B) with 2 different embeddings (Qwen3, MiniLM).\n\n**Key results**: The pairwise SCS-confusion correlation shows significant effects for Qwen3 embeddings across the tasks. Looking at the experiment list from the review context, I'm checking which ones actually made it into the paper—MAVEN and DocRED confusability studies are covered across multiple tables, the interaction analysis is there, but I need to verify coverage for the SDGD variants, ablations, and the embedding validation work. I've confirmed the final experiment on CS validation is complete, so all the work is accounted for. Now I need to move forward with dispatching the remaining reviewers.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17131,"cache_read_input_tokens":44649,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17131},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8789515-c41b-4a57-af8d-19a8b3cf59ba","uuid":"de41d474-484e-4ff9-a48f-b1e8da75902d"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Tqj8N137QQw8yWYt7KUP1s","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"论文已读完，实验设计参考文件已读。现在按 dream-review-full dispatch 模板，并行启动两位 Reviewer。"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17131,"cache_read_input_tokens":44649,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17131},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8789515-c41b-4a57-af8d-19a8b3cf59ba","uuid":"46808e7d-e257-40f2-8b16-dd446dfdc46e"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Tqj8N137QQw8yWYt7KUP1s","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01256649ExCeCSqzEGrx2uUG","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a peer review of a machine learning paper. Your focus is **methodology and experimental rigor**. You must produce an independent review — do NOT attempt to coordinate with or reference any other reviewer.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Is Schema Complexity One-Dimensional? A Controlled Empirical Study of Type Count, Confusability, and Their Interaction in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction\"\n\n**Core question**: Is schema complexity one-dimensional, or do distinct dimensions (type count, type confusability) degrade LLM extraction through separable mechanisms?\n\n**Setup**: 3 IE tasks (Few-NERD NER with 66 types, MAVEN Event Detection with 168 types, DocRED Relation Extraction with 96 types), 2 LLMs (Qwen3-32B primary, LLaMA-3.3-70B for cross-model), 2 embedding spaces (Qwen3, MiniLM).\n\n**Key metric**: Schema Confusability Score (SCS) = cosine similarity between type definition embeddings. Pairwise SCS between type pairs; Batch Confusability Score (BCS) = per-type average of SCS across co-present types.\n\n**Four findings**:\n1. Pairwise confusability predicts type confusion, but model/embedding-dependent. Qwen3 on Few-NERD: r=0.156 (p<10^-11). MiniLM on MAVEN: r=0.115 (p<10^-42). LLaMA-3.3-70B on Few-NERD: r=0.042 (NS, p=0.068). Only 3/7 tested cells significant.\n2. Count and confusability act through distinguishable channels: count drives entity omission (miss rate 0.28→0.55 monotonically), confusability drives type substitution (4.8× extreme ratio, 1.5× random). 2×2 ANOVA: count F=7.82 p=0.008, BCS F=2.93 p=0.094 (marginal), interaction F=0.31 p=0.582 (NS).\n3. Per-type confusability aggregation introduces centrality confound on MAVEN (positive correlation ρ=+0.195/+0.212 — opposite of confusability hypothesis). Pairwise analysis avoids this.\n4. Confusability-minimizing decomposition (SDGD) fails: SDGD-zero Δ=-4.7pp (Few-NERD), -7.3pp (MAVEN) vs single-pass. SDGD-cal Δ=-3.3pp, -5.3pp. SDGD ≈ random batching (NS). Per-type splitting worst. Root cause: decomposition removes disambiguation context.\n\n**Additional results**:\n- DocRED: complete null result across all confusability analyses (flat count curve, no BCS signal). Framed as boundary condition.\n- Embedding proxy validation: Qwen3 SCS vs empirical confusion ρ=0.125 (p=0.031), false positive rate 42%. MiniLM ρ=0.098 (p=0.082, NS).\n- Count curves differ by task: decreasing (Few-NERD), increasing (MAVEN), flat (DocRED).\n- Error taxonomy: spurious extraction 45%, missed entity 25%, type substitution 18%, boundary error 12%. Type substitution is confusability-related but NOT the dominant error mode.\n- Full method comparison (Few-NERD F1): single-pass 0.414 > SDGD-cal 0.381 > random 0.374 ≈ SDGD-zero 0.367 > per-type 0.330.\n\n**Paper structure**: 13 pages. Sections: Intro, Related Work, Experimental Setup, Finding 1 (pairwise confusability), Finding 2 (count/confusability channels), Finding 3 (centrality confound), Finding 4 (decomposition fails), Discussion, Conclusion, Appendix A (SDGD detailed results). 5 references. Tables 1-25.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe full imagined experiment list includes these experiments, all of which appear to be covered in the paper:\n- exp-004: MAVEN Full Confusability Study\n- exp-005: DocRED Confusability Study  \n- exp-006: Count × Confusability 2×2 Interaction (n=48, 12 per cell)\n- exp-007: Systematic Error Taxonomy by BCS Bins\n- exp-012: LLaMA-3.3-70B Multi-Model Validation\n- exp-008: SDGD-zero on Few-NERD\n- exp-009: SDGD-zero on MAVEN\n- exp-010: SDGD-cal on Few-NERD\n- exp-011: SDGD-cal on MAVEN\n- exp-013: Random Batching Ablation\n- exp-014: Per-Type Splitting Ablation\n- exp-015: Embedding SCS vs Empirical Confusion Validation\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on **methodology and experimental rigor**. Address ALL of the following:\n\n1. **Experimental Design**: Are the controlled experiments well-designed? Is the factorial design adequate? Are confounds properly controlled?\n\n2. **Statistical Rigor**: Are the statistical tests appropriate? Are effect sizes reported alongside p-values? Is multiple comparison correction applied? Are sample sizes adequate?\n\n3. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are hyperparameters, prompts, sampling procedures described?\n\n4. **Internal Validity**: Could alternative explanations account for the findings? Are the causal claims justified?\n\n5. **Coverage vs Design**: The paper claims \"first controlled empirical study\" — assess whether the experimental coverage (3 tasks, 2 models, 2 embeddings) is sufficient or if gaps weaken the claims.\n\n6. **Metric Design**: Is SCS (cosine similarity of type definition embeddings) a well-motivated confusability measure? Does the BCS aggregation make sense?\n\n7. **Negative Results**: Are the negative results (SDGD failure, LLaMA null, DocRED null) properly interpreted? Could they be artifacts of experimental design?\n\n8. **Specific Weaknesses**: Identify 3-5 concrete methodological weaknesses with specific suggestions for improvement.\n\n## Output Format\n\nProvide your review in this exact structure:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methodology & Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences summarizing the paper's contribution from a methods perspective]\n\n### Strengths\n[Bulleted list, 3-5 items]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Bulleted list, 3-5 items, each with specific evidence and actionable suggestion]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[3-5 specific questions]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Any minor methodological concerns]\n\n### Confidence Score\n[1-5 scale: 1=not confident, 5=very confident]\n\n### Preliminary Rating\n[1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept]\nInclude a one-sentence justification.\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is a paper with many negative/null results — evaluate whether the negative results themselves are informative contributions or just failures.","model":"sonnet"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17131,"cache_read_input_tokens":44649,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17131},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8789515-c41b-4a57-af8d-19a8b3cf59ba","uuid":"6e170afa-62cb-4086-b394-637f4ad5afd9"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a41904d0543cf3ace","tool_use_id":"toolu_01256649ExCeCSqzEGrx2uUG","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a peer review of a machine learning paper. Your focus is **methodology and experimental rigor**. You must produce an independent review — do NOT attempt to coordinate with or reference any other reviewer.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Is Schema Complexity One-Dimensional? A Controlled Empirical Study of Type Count, Confusability, and Their Interaction in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction\"\n\n**Core question**: Is schema complexity one-dimensional, or do distinct dimensions (type count, type confusability) degrade LLM extraction through separable mechanisms?\n\n**Setup**: 3 IE tasks (Few-NERD NER with 66 types, MAVEN Event Detection with 168 types, DocRED Relation Extraction with 96 types), 2 LLMs (Qwen3-32B primary, LLaMA-3.3-70B for cross-model), 2 embedding spaces (Qwen3, MiniLM).\n\n**Key metric**: Schema Confusability Score (SCS) = cosine similarity between type definition embeddings. Pairwise SCS between type pairs; Batch Confusability Score (BCS) = per-type average of SCS across co-present types.\n\n**Four findings**:\n1. Pairwise confusability predicts type confusion, but model/embedding-dependent. Qwen3 on Few-NERD: r=0.156 (p<10^-11). MiniLM on MAVEN: r=0.115 (p<10^-42). LLaMA-3.3-70B on Few-NERD: r=0.042 (NS, p=0.068). Only 3/7 tested cells significant.\n2. Count and confusability act through distinguishable channels: count drives entity omission (miss rate 0.28→0.55 monotonically), confusability drives type substitution (4.8× extreme ratio, 1.5× random). 2×2 ANOVA: count F=7.82 p=0.008, BCS F=2.93 p=0.094 (marginal), interaction F=0.31 p=0.582 (NS).\n3. Per-type confusability aggregation introduces centrality confound on MAVEN (positive correlation ρ=+0.195/+0.212 — opposite of confusability hypothesis). Pairwise analysis avoids this.\n4. Confusability-minimizing decomposition (SDGD) fails: SDGD-zero Δ=-4.7pp (Few-NERD), -7.3pp (MAVEN) vs single-pass. SDGD-cal Δ=-3.3pp, -5.3pp. SDGD ≈ random batching (NS). Per-type splitting worst. Root cause: decomposition removes disambiguation context.\n\n**Additional results**:\n- DocRED: complete null result across all confusability analyses (flat count curve, no BCS signal). Framed as boundary condition.\n- Embedding proxy validation: Qwen3 SCS vs empirical confusion ρ=0.125 (p=0.031), false positive rate 42%. MiniLM ρ=0.098 (p=0.082, NS).\n- Count curves differ by task: decreasing (Few-NERD), increasing (MAVEN), flat (DocRED).\n- Error taxonomy: spurious extraction 45%, missed entity 25%, type substitution 18%, boundary error 12%. Type substitution is confusability-related but NOT the dominant error mode.\n- Full method comparison (Few-NERD F1): single-pass 0.414 > SDGD-cal 0.381 > random 0.374 ≈ SDGD-zero 0.367 > per-type 0.330.\n\n**Paper structure**: 13 pages. Sections: Intro, Related Work, Experimental Setup, Finding 1 (pairwise confusability), Finding 2 (count/confusability channels), Finding 3 (centrality confound), Finding 4 (decomposition fails), Discussion, Conclusion, Appendix A (SDGD detailed results). 5 references. Tables 1-25.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe full imagined experiment list includes these experiments, all of which appear to be covered in the paper:\n- exp-004: MAVEN Full Confusability Study\n- exp-005: DocRED Confusability Study  \n- exp-006: Count × Confusability 2×2 Interaction (n=48, 12 per cell)\n- exp-007: Systematic Error Taxonomy by BCS Bins\n- exp-012: LLaMA-3.3-70B Multi-Model Validation\n- exp-008: SDGD-zero on Few-NERD\n- exp-009: SDGD-zero on MAVEN\n- exp-010: SDGD-cal on Few-NERD\n- exp-011: SDGD-cal on MAVEN\n- exp-013: Random Batching Ablation\n- exp-014: Per-Type Splitting Ablation\n- exp-015: Embedding SCS vs Empirical Confusion Validation\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on **methodology and experimental rigor**. Address ALL of the following:\n\n1. **Experimental Design**: Are the controlled experiments well-designed? Is the factorial design adequate? Are confounds properly controlled?\n\n2. **Statistical Rigor**: Are the statistical tests appropriate? Are effect sizes reported alongside p-values? Is multiple comparison correction applied? Are sample sizes adequate?\n\n3. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are hyperparameters, prompts, sampling procedures described?\n\n4. **Internal Validity**: Could alternative explanations account for the findings? Are the causal claims justified?\n\n5. **Coverage vs Design**: The paper claims \"first controlled empirical study\" — assess whether the experimental coverage (3 tasks, 2 models, 2 embeddings) is sufficient or if gaps weaken the claims.\n\n6. **Metric Design**: Is SCS (cosine similarity of type definition embeddings) a well-motivated confusability measure? Does the BCS aggregation make sense?\n\n7. **Negative Results**: Are the negative results (SDGD failure, LLaMA null, DocRED null) properly interpreted? Could they be artifacts of experimental design?\n\n8. **Specific Weaknesses**: Identify 3-5 concrete methodological weaknesses with specific suggestions for improvement.\n\n## Output Format\n\nProvide your review in this exact structure:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methodology & Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences summarizing the paper's contribution from a methods perspective]\n\n### Strengths\n[Bulleted list, 3-5 items]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Bulleted list, 3-5 items, each with specific evidence and actionable suggestion]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[3-5 specific questions]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Any minor methodological concerns]\n\n### Confidence Score\n[1-5 scale: 1=not confident, 5=very confident]\n\n### Preliminary Rating\n[1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept]\nInclude a one-sentence justification.\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is a paper with many negative/null results — evaluate whether the negative results themselves are informative contributions or just failures.","uuid":"b260ee7c-66f6-4170-a1ec-a205f87136ef","session_id":"a8789515-c41b-4a57-af8d-19a8b3cf59ba"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a peer review of a machine learning paper. Your focus is **methodology and experimental rigor**. You must produce an independent review — do NOT attempt to coordinate with or reference any other reviewer.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Is Schema Complexity One-Dimensional? A Controlled Empirical Study of Type Count, Confusability, and Their Interaction in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction\"\n\n**Core question**: Is schema complexity one-dimensional, or do distinct dimensions (type count, type confusability) degrade LLM extraction through separable mechanisms?\n\n**Setup**: 3 IE tasks (Few-NERD NER with 66 types, MAVEN Event Detection with 168 types, DocRED Relation Extraction with 96 types), 2 LLMs (Qwen3-32B primary, LLaMA-3.3-70B for cross-model), 2 embedding spaces (Qwen3, MiniLM).\n\n**Key metric**: Schema Confusability Score (SCS) = cosine similarity between type definition embeddings. Pairwise SCS between type pairs; Batch Confusability Score (BCS) = per-type average of SCS across co-present types.\n\n**Four findings**:\n1. Pairwise confusability predicts type confusion, but model/embedding-dependent. Qwen3 on Few-NERD: r=0.156 (p<10^-11). MiniLM on MAVEN: r=0.115 (p<10^-42). LLaMA-3.3-70B on Few-NERD: r=0.042 (NS, p=0.068). Only 3/7 tested cells significant.\n2. Count and confusability act through distinguishable channels: count drives entity omission (miss rate 0.28→0.55 monotonically), confusability drives type substitution (4.8× extreme ratio, 1.5× random). 2×2 ANOVA: count F=7.82 p=0.008, BCS F=2.93 p=0.094 (marginal), interaction F=0.31 p=0.582 (NS).\n3. Per-type confusability aggregation introduces centrality confound on MAVEN (positive correlation ρ=+0.195/+0.212 — opposite of confusability hypothesis). Pairwise analysis avoids this.\n4. Confusability-minimizing decomposition (SDGD) fails: SDGD-zero Δ=-4.7pp (Few-NERD), -7.3pp (MAVEN) vs single-pass. SDGD-cal Δ=-3.3pp, -5.3pp. SDGD ≈ random batching (NS). Per-type splitting worst. Root cause: decomposition removes disambiguation context.\n\n**Additional results**:\n- DocRED: complete null result across all confusability analyses (flat count curve, no BCS signal). Framed as boundary condition.\n- Embedding proxy validation: Qwen3 SCS vs empirical confusion ρ=0.125 (p=0.031), false positive rate 42%. MiniLM ρ=0.098 (p=0.082, NS).\n- Count curves differ by task: decreasing (Few-NERD), increasing (MAVEN), flat (DocRED).\n- Error taxonomy: spurious extraction 45%, missed entity 25%, type substitution 18%, boundary error 12%. Type substitution is confusability-related but NOT the dominant error mode.\n- Full method comparison (Few-NERD F1): single-pass 0.414 > SDGD-cal 0.381 > random 0.374 ≈ SDGD-zero 0.367 > per-type 0.330.\n\n**Paper structure**: 13 pages. Sections: Intro, Related Work, Experimental Setup, Finding 1 (pairwise confusability), Finding 2 (count/confusability channels), Finding 3 (centrality confound), Finding 4 (decomposition fails), Discussion, Conclusion, Appendix A (SDGD detailed results). 5 references. Tables 1-25.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe full imagined experiment list includes these experiments, all of which appear to be covered in the paper:\n- exp-004: MAVEN Full Confusability Study\n- exp-005: DocRED Confusability Study  \n- exp-006: Count × Confusability 2×2 Interaction (n=48, 12 per cell)\n- exp-007: Systematic Error Taxonomy by BCS Bins\n- exp-012: LLaMA-3.3-70B Multi-Model Validation\n- exp-008: SDGD-zero on Few-NERD\n- exp-009: SDGD-zero on MAVEN\n- exp-010: SDGD-cal on Few-NERD\n- exp-011: SDGD-cal on MAVEN\n- exp-013: Random Batching Ablation\n- exp-014: Per-Type Splitting Ablation\n- exp-015: Embedding SCS vs Empirical Confusion Validation\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on **methodology and experimental rigor**. Address ALL of the following:\n\n1. **Experimental Design**: Are the controlled experiments well-designed? Is the factorial design adequate? Are confounds properly controlled?\n\n2. **Statistical Rigor**: Are the statistical tests appropriate? Are effect sizes reported alongside p-values? Is multiple comparison correction applied? Are sample sizes adequate?\n\n3. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are hyperparameters, prompts, sampling procedures described?\n\n4. **Internal Validity**: Could alternative explanations account for the findings? Are the causal claims justified?\n\n5. **Coverage vs Design**: The paper claims \"first controlled empirical study\" — assess whether the experimental coverage (3 tasks, 2 models, 2 embeddings) is sufficient or if gaps weaken the claims.\n\n6. **Metric Design**: Is SCS (cosine similarity of type definition embeddings) a well-motivated confusability measure? Does the BCS aggregation make sense?\n\n7. **Negative Results**: Are the negative results (SDGD failure, LLaMA null, DocRED null) properly interpreted? Could they be artifacts of experimental design?\n\n8. **Specific Weaknesses**: Identify 3-5 concrete methodological weaknesses with specific suggestions for improvement.\n\n## Output Format\n\nProvide your review in this exact structure:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methodology & Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences summarizing the paper's contribution from a methods perspective]\n\n### Strengths\n[Bulleted list, 3-5 items]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Bulleted list, 3-5 items, each with specific evidence and actionable suggestion]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[3-5 specific questions]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Any minor methodological concerns]\n\n### Confidence Score\n[1-5 scale: 1=not confident, 5=very confident]\n\n### Preliminary Rating\n[1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept]\nInclude a one-sentence justification.\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is a paper with many negative/null results — evaluate whether the 