tive results (SDGD fails, model-dependence) change how the community should approach schema decomposition? Is the impact sufficient for a top venue?\n\n3. **Related Work Adequacy**: Only 5 references are cited. Is this sufficient? What major related work is missing? Consider:\n   - Schema complexity in supervised IE\n   - Label confusion/confusability in classification literature\n   - Other LLM IE methods beyond UniNER/GoLLIE/LLM-PEE\n   - Prompt engineering for structured extraction\n   - Error analysis in NER/RE/ED\n\n4. **Presentation Quality**: Is the paper well-organized? Is the findings-based structure effective? Are claims properly hedged given the evidence? Is the writing clear?\n\n5. **Claim-Evidence Alignment**: For each of the 4 findings, assess whether the evidence fully supports the claim as stated. Flag any overclaiming or underclaiming.\n\n6. **Positioning**: The paper positions itself as \"the first controlled empirical study\" — is this claim defensible? Does it sufficiently differentiate from prior empirical observations in UniNER/GoLLIE/LLM-PEE?\n\n7. **Venue Fit**: Is this paper appropriate for a top ML/NLP venue? Would it fit better at a workshop, Findings track, or main conference?\n\n## Output Format\n\nProvide your review in this exact structure:\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty, Significance & Presentation\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences summarizing the paper's contribution from a novelty/significance perspective]\n\n### Strengths\n[Bulleted list, 3-5 items]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Bulleted list, 3-5 items, each with specific evidence and actionable suggestion]\n\n### Missing Related Work\n[Specific papers/areas that should be cited and discussed]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[3-5 specific questions]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Presentation, writing, or formatting concerns]\n\n### Confidence Score\n[1-5 scale: 1=not confident, 5=very confident]\n\n### Preliminary Rating\n[1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept]\nInclude a one-sentence justification.\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Remember that empirical studies with well-designed negative results can be valuable contributions — evaluate on those terms, not just on whether the paper proposes a new method that beats baselines.","uuid":"77e96806-0893-4faa-a6cb-87724748c473","session_id":"a8789515-c41b-4a57-af8d-19a8b3cf59ba"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a peer review of a machine learning paper. Your focus is **novelty, significance, related work, and presentation**. You must produce an independent review — do NOT attempt to coordinate with or reference any other reviewer.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Is Schema Complexity One-Dimensional? A Controlled Empirical Study of Type Count, Confusability, and Their Interaction in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction\"\n\n**Core question**: Is schema complexity one-dimensional, or do distinct dimensions (type count, type confusability) degrade LLM extraction through separable mechanisms?\n\n**Setup**: 3 IE tasks (Few-NERD NER with 66 types, MAVEN Event Detection with 168 types, DocRED Relation Extraction with 96 types), 2 LLMs (Qwen3-32B primary, LLaMA-3.3-70B for cross-model), 2 embedding spaces (Qwen3, MiniLM).\n\n**Key metric**: Schema Confusability Score (SCS) = cosine similarity between type definition embeddings. Pairwise SCS between type pairs; Batch Confusability Score (BCS) = per-type average of SCS across co-present types.\n\n**Four findings**:\n1. Pairwise confusability predicts type confusion, but model/embedding-dependent. Qwen3 on Few-NERD: r=0.156 (p<10^-11). MiniLM on MAVEN: r=0.115 (p<10^-42). LLaMA-3.3-70B on Few-NERD: r=0.042 (NS, p=0.068). Only 3/7 tested cells significant.\n2. Count and confusability act through distinguishable channels: count drives entity omission (miss rate 0.28→0.55), confusability drives type substitution (4.8× extreme ratio, 1.5× random). 2×2 ANOVA: count significant, BCS marginal (p=0.094), interaction NS (p=0.582).\n3. Per-type confusability aggregation introduces centrality confound on MAVEN (positive correlation — opposite of confusability hypothesis). Pairwise analysis avoids this.\n4. Confusability-minimizing decomposition (SDGD) fails: all variants worse than single-pass. Δ=-3.3pp to -7.3pp. SDGD ≈ random batching. Root cause: decomposition removes disambiguation context that LLMs use for comparative type reasoning.\n\n**Additional key details**:\n- DocRED yields complete null results — framed as boundary condition (confusability effects require type definitions to occupy significant prompt fraction).\n- Count curves differ dramatically by task: decreasing (Few-NERD), increasing (MAVEN), flat (DocRED).\n- Embedding proxy validation: SCS explains only ~1.6% of confusion variance (ρ=0.125). 42% false positive rate.\n- Error taxonomy: type substitution (the confusability-related error) accounts for only 18% of errors; spurious extraction dominates at 45%.\n- Cross-model: 4× effect size difference between Qwen3 (r=0.156) and LLaMA (r=0.042).\n- Full method comparison table (Few-NERD F1): single-pass 0.414 > SDGD-cal 0.381 > random 0.374 ≈ SDGD-zero 0.367 > per-type 0.330.\n\n**Related work cited** (only 5 references):\n[1] UniNER (Zhou et al., ICLR 2024) — per-type splitting for NER\n[2] GoLLIE (Sainz et al., ICLR 2024) — structured type definitions\n[3] LLM-PEE (Zaratiana et al., arXiv 2024) — embedding similarity-based grouping\n[4] Few-NERD dataset (Ding et al., ACL-IJCNLP 2021)\n[5] MAVEN dataset (Wang et al., EMNLP 2020)\n\n**Paper structure**: 13 pages. Introduction → Related Work (short, 3 paragraphs) → Experimental Setup → Findings 1-4 → Discussion → Conclusion → Appendix A. 25 tables.\n\n**Target venue**: Top ML venue (e.g., EMNLP/ACL), ~25% acceptance rate, 6-point scale.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on **novelty, significance, related work coverage, and presentation quality**. Address ALL of the following:\n\n1. **Novelty**: How novel is the research question? How novel are the findings? Is the \"controlled empirical study\" framing itself a contribution?\n\n2. **Significance**: Who benefits from these findings? Do the negative results (SDGD fails, model-dependence) change how the community should approach schema decomposition? Is the impact sufficient for a top venue?\n\n3. **Related Work Adequacy**: Only 5 references are cited. Is this sufficient? What major related work is missing? Consider:\n   - Schema complexity in supervised IE\n   - Label confusion/confusability in classification literature\n   - Other LLM IE methods beyond UniNER/GoLLIE/LLM-PEE\n   - Prompt engineering for structured extraction\n   - Error analysis in NER/RE/ED\n\n4. **Presentation Quality**: Is the paper well-organized? Is the findings-based structure effective? Are claims properly hedged given the evidence? Is the writing clear?\n\n5. **Claim-Evidence Alignment**: For each of the 4 findings, assess whether the evidence fully supports the claim as stated. Flag any overclaiming or underclaiming.\n\n6. **Positioning**: The paper positions itself as \"the first controlled empirical study\" — is this claim defensible? Does it sufficiently differentiate from prior empirical observations in UniNER/GoLLIE/LLM-PEE?\n\n7. **Venue Fit**: Is this paper appropriate for a top ML/NLP venue? Would it fit better at a workshop, Findings track, or main conference?\n\n## Output Format\n\nProvide your review in this exact structure:\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty, Significance & Presentation\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences summarizing the paper's contribution from a novelty/significance perspective]\n\n### Strengths\n[Bulleted list, 3-5 items]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Bulleted list, 3-5 items, each with specific evidence and actionable suggestion]\n\n### Missing Related Work\n[Specific papers/areas that should be cited and discussed]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[3-5 specific questions]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Presentation, writing, or formatting concerns]\n\n### Confidence Score\n[1-5 scale: 1=not confident, 5=very confident]\n\n### Preliminary Rating\n[1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept]\nInclude a one-sentence justification.\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Remember that empirical studies with well-designed negative results can be valuable contributions — evaluate on those terms, not just on whether the paper proposes a new method that beats baselines."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NXUEE17zWwbiboFtV1tHgN","session_id":"a8789515-c41b-4a57-af8d-19a8b3cf59ba","uuid":"440a39d4-e96e-4d15-a44e-abe7c902375c","timestamp":"2026-05-15T12:34:09.940Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a41904d0543cf3ace","tool_use_id":"toolu_01256649ExCeCSqzEGrx2uUG","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","usage":{"total_tokens":15731,"tool_uses":0,"duration_ms":51615},"uuid":"e0717639-2ebf-4f42-9632-d361e71e6131","session_id":"a8789515-c41b-4a57-af8d-19a8b3cf59ba"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01256649ExCeCSqzEGrx2uUG","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## R1 Review: Methodology & Experiments\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper investigates whether schema complexity in LLM-based information extraction is one-dimensional or multi-dimensional, using controlled factorial experiments across three IE tasks and two models. The central methodological contribution is the operationalization of \"confusability\" via embedding-space cosine similarity and its separation from type count as an independent complexity axis. The paper reports mostly null or weak results for confusability effects and a negative result for confusability-minimizing decomposition, framing these as informative boundary conditions rather than failures.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Factorial design is appropriate for the core question.** The 2×2 count × BCS design (exp-006, n=48, 12 per cell) is a principled approach for separating additive from interactive effects. The explicit ANOVA framing with F-statistics and p-values reported for main effects and interaction term is more rigorous than the correlational approaches common in prompt engineering papers.\n\n- **Controlled ablation coverage is unusually thorough.** Five decomposition variants (single-pass, SDGD-zero, SDGD-cal, random batching, per-type) with results on two datasets gives a clear picture of the decomposition failure mode. The inclusion of a random batching baseline (exp-013) is particularly important — without it, the SDGD failure would be uninterpretable.\n\n- **The centrality confound identification (Finding 3) is a genuine methodological contribution.** The ρ=+0.195/+0.212 sign reversal on MAVEN is an observable artifact with a plausible causal mechanism (high-frequency types have more co-occurrence partners, inflating BCS). This is a concrete, reproducible warning for future work using aggregate confusability scores.\n\n- **Embedding proxy validation (exp-015) is the right experiment to run before interpreting all other SCS-based results.** Reporting the 42% false positive rate and the MiniLM ρ=0.098 (NS) result honestly, before using SCS as a predictor throughout the paper, reflects appropriate epistemic caution.\n\n- **The SDGD failure explanation (loss of disambiguation context) is testable and specific**, and the authors correctly note that SDGD-cal ≈ SDGD-zero ≈ random (all NS) makes the null result robust rather than contingent on a single implementation choice.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **Multiple comparison correction is absent and the selective significance pattern is not adequately flagged.** The paper reports 7 SCS-confusion correlation tests (Finding 1) and highlights 3 as significant. With no Bonferroni or FDR correction, the expected number of false positives at α=0.05 is 0.35, making 3 discoveries unsurprising. The paper treats the 3/7 pattern as evidence of \"model/embedding-dependence\" rather than noise. Correction is essential: if Bonferroni-corrected α=0.007, the r=0.042 (p=0.068) and r=0.098 (p=0.082) results are already NS, and the question becomes whether the remaining significant results survive. *Actionable suggestion*: Apply FDR correction across all pairwise and aggregate SCS tests and restate which findings survive.\n\n- **The 2×2 ANOVA (exp-006) has a serious cell-construction confound.** \"High confusability\" and \"low confusability\" batches are constructed by selecting type subsets from the same dataset. This means cells differ not only in BCS but systematically in which types are included — if high-BCS types are also rarer, more abstract, or harder in other unmeasured ways, the BCS F-statistic is confounded. The paper does not report whether type frequency, instance count, or annotation density are balanced across cells. *Actionable suggestion*: Report type frequency distributions and instance counts per ANOVA cell, or use stratified sampling that explicitly matches these covariates.\n\n- **The SCS metric conflates two distinct constructs without justification.** Cosine similarity between type *definition* embeddings measures semantic proximity in the embedding space of definitions, which is one possible operationalization of confusability. However, a model's tendency to confuse two types depends on how those types appear in *context*, not just definition similarity. The 42% false positive rate (exp-015) is direct evidence this is an imperfect proxy. The paper does not consider alternative operationalizations (e.g., prompt-based discriminability, definition length, co-occurrence frequency in training data) or discuss when definition-embedding SCS is and is not a valid proxy. *Actionable suggestion*: Include at minimum a comparison of Qwen3-embedding SCS vs. a simple lexical overlap baseline between type names/definitions, to anchor the embedding-space metric's value-add.\n\n- **The DocRED null result is explained post-hoc without experimental support.** The paper frames DocRED as a \"boundary condition\" where the proposed mechanisms do not operate, attributing this to relational extraction's context-dependence. This is plausible but not tested: no ablation removes the proposed confound (e.g., testing RE with entity type cues suppressed) nor is there any analysis of *why* count curves are flat for RE. The null result for an entire task type means the paper's findings may not generalize to RE at all, yet the title and abstract claim generality across \"LLM information extraction.\" *Actionable suggestion*: Either narrow the scope claims explicitly to span/mention-level extraction tasks, or add a diagnostic analysis of DocRED errors to test whether the proposed mechanisms are simply absent or masked.\n\n- **Effect sizes are reported inconsistently and are uniformly small, but this is not discussed.** r=0.156 (Finding 1, Qwen3/Few-NERD) explains ~2.4% of variance. The 4.8× \"extreme confusability\" ratio (Finding 2) comes from a selected extreme-vs-random comparison rather than the ANOVA; the ANOVA BCS F=2.93 p=0.094 is marginal and not significant. The miss-rate increase 0.28→0.55 is the strongest effect in the paper, but this is a count effect, not a confusability effect. The confusability effects that survive are consistently small. The paper does not compute power for the ANOVA nor discuss whether the study was adequately powered to detect plausible effect sizes. *Actionable suggestion*: Compute post-hoc power for the 2×2 ANOVA given observed effect sizes, and add an explicit section discussing practical significance vs. statistical significance of confusability effects.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Cell balance in exp-006**: How were the 12 schema subsets per ANOVA cell selected? Specifically, are type frequency (instance counts per type), type abstractness, and annotation density matched across high-BCS and low-BCS cells? If cells were selected purely on BCS with no covariate matching, the ANOVA results are confounded.\n\n2. **Multiple testing**: Across all reported correlation tests (SCS vs. confusion, BCS vs. miss rate, BCS vs. substitution rate, etc.), how many total hypothesis tests are conducted in the paper? If the authors apply FDR correction, which results survive at q<0.05?\n\n3. **LLaMA-3.3-70B scope**: The cross-model validation (exp-012) uses LLaMA only on Few-NERD with Qwen3 embeddings. Given that LLaMA produces an NS result (r=0.042, p=0.068), is it possible the Qwen3 SCS metric is simply better calibrated to Qwen3's own confusion patterns? Was LLaMA evaluated with LLaMA-space embeddings?\n\n4. **SDGD context hypothesis**: The proposed explanation for SDGD failure — that decomposition removes disambiguation context — predicts that adding explicit cross-type contrast examples to each sub-batch should recover performance. Is this prediction tested anywhere? Without a direct test, the explanation remains a hypothesis rather than a finding.\n\n5. **Prompt and sampling details**: For reproducibility, what is the exact prompt template used for single-pass extraction across the three tasks? Were outputs generated with greedy decoding or sampling? What temperature/top-p settings were used? These details appear absent from the main paper and are essential for replication.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n- Table count (25 tables) is excessive for a 13-page paper; many tables appear to contain results that could be consolidated, making it difficult to identify primary evidence for each finding.\n- The paper has 5 references, which is critically sparse for a \"related work\" section claiming this is the \"first controlled empirical study\" — this claim requires engagement with prior work on schema reduction, type hierarchy, and IE with LLMs.\n- The error taxonomy (45% spurious, 25% missed, 18% substitution, 12% boundary) is reported globally but not broken down by count condition or confusability bin, which would directly connect the taxonomy to Findings 1 and 2.\n- \"Training-free\" in the title is accurate but could be perceived as a limitation rather than a feature; the paper would benefit from one sentence justifying why training-free is the appropriate scope.\n- p<10^-11 and p<10^-42 for n-driven Pearson correlations indicate extremely large sample sizes — the large n makes even trivially small r values significant, which reinforces the concern that effect sizes rather than p-values should be the primary evidence.\n\n---\n\n### Confidence Score\n\n**4 / 5** — The experimental setup is described with enough specificity to evaluate the design, though absence of prompt templates and cell-construction details for the ANOVA introduce residual uncertainty.\n\n---\n\n### Preliminary Rating\n\n**3 / 6 — Weak Reject**\n\nThe paper addresses a genuine and underexplored question with appropriate factorial methodology and commendable transparency about null results, but the absence of multiple-comparison correction across a selective 3/7 significance pattern, unverified covariate balance in the key ANOVA, and a complete null result for one of three tasks without diagnostic follow-up leave the primary positive claims insufficiently supported relative to the claim of a \"controlled empirical study.\""},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a41904d0543cf3ace (use SendMessage with to: 'a41904d0543cf3ace' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 18109\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 51619</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a8789515-c41b-4a57-af8d-19a8b3cf59ba","uuid":"36b6da81-b59b-42b9-b3ae-e6a06e509ed3","timestamp":"2026-05-15T12:34:34.852Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a peer review of a machine learning paper. Your focus is **methodology and experimental rigor**. You must produce an independent review — do NOT attempt to coordinate with or reference any other reviewer.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: \"Is Schema Complexity One-Dimensional? A Controlled Empirical Study of Type Count, Confusability, and Their Interaction in Training-Free LLM Information Extraction\"\n\n**Core question**: Is schema complexity one-dimensional, or do distinct dimensions (type count, type confusability) degrade LLM extraction through separable mechanisms?\n\n**Setup**: 3 IE tasks (Few-NERD NER with 66 types, MAVEN Event Detection with 168 types, DocRED Relation Extraction with 96 types), 2 LLMs (Qwen3-32B primary, LLaMA-3.3-70B for cross-model), 2 embedding spaces (Qwen3, MiniLM).\n\n**Key metric**: Schema Confusability Score (SCS) = cosine similarity between type definition embeddings. Pairwise SCS between type pairs; Batch Confusability Score (BCS) = per-type average of SCS across co-present types.\n\n**Four findings**:\n1. Pairwise confusability predicts type confusion, but model/embedding-dependent. Qwen3 on Few-NERD: r=0.156 (p<10^-11). MiniLM on MAVEN: r=0.115 (p<10^-42). LLaMA-3.3-70B on Few-NERD: r=0.042 (NS, p=0.068). Only 3/7 tested cells significant.\n2. Count and confusability act through distinguishable channels: count drives entity omission (miss rate 0.28→0.55 monotonically), confusability drives type substitution (4.8× extreme ratio, 1.5× random). 2×2 ANOVA: count F=7.82 p=0.008, BCS F=2.93 p=0.094 (marginal), interaction F=0.31 p=0.582 (NS).\n3. Per-type confusability aggregation introduces centrality confound on MAVEN (positive correlation ρ=+0.195/+0.212 — opposite of confusability hypothesis). Pairwise analysis avoids this.\n4. Confusability-minimizing decomposition (SDGD) fails: SDGD-zero Δ=-4.7pp (Few-NERD), -7.3pp (MAVEN) vs single-pass. SDGD-cal Δ=-3.3pp, -5.3pp. SDGD ≈ random batching (NS). Per-type splitting worst. Root cause: decomposition removes disambiguation context.\n\n**Additional results**:\n- DocRED: complete null result across all confusability analyses (flat count curve, no BCS signal). Framed as boundary condition.\n- Embedding proxy validation: Qwen3 SCS vs empirical confusion ρ=0.125 (p=0.031), false positive rate 42%. MiniLM ρ=0.098 (p=0.082, NS).\n- Count curves differ by task: decreasing (Few-NERD), increasing (MAVEN), flat (DocRED).\n- Error taxonomy: spurious extraction 45%, missed entity 25%, type substitution 18%, boundary error 12%. Type substitution is confusability-related but NOT the dominant error mode.\n- Full method comparison (Few-NERD F1): single-pass 0.414 > SDGD-cal 0.381 > random 0.374 ≈ SDGD-zero 0.367 > per-type 0.330.\n\n**Paper structure**: 13 pages. Sections: Intro, Related Work, Experimental Setup, Finding 1 (pairwise confusability), Finding 2 (count/confusability channels), Finding 3 (centrality confound), Finding 4 (decomposition fails), Discussion, Conclusion, Appendix A (SDGD detailed results). 5 references. Tables 1-25.\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe full imagined experiment list includes these experiments, all of which appear to be covered in the paper:\n- exp-004: MAVEN Full Confusability Study\n- exp-005: DocRED Confusability Study  \n- exp-006: Count × Confusability 2×2 Interaction (n=48, 12 per cell)\n- exp-007: Systematic Error Taxonomy by BCS Bins\n- exp-012: LLaMA-3.3-70B Multi-Model Validation\n- exp-008: SDGD-zero on Few-NERD\n- exp-009: SDGD-zero on MAVEN\n- exp-010: SDGD-cal on Few-NERD\n- exp-011: SDGD-cal on MAVEN\n- exp-013: Random Batching Ablation\n- exp-014: Per-Type Splitting Ablation\n- exp-015: Embedding SCS vs Empirical Confusion Validation\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on **methodology and experimental rigor**. Address ALL of the following:\n\n1. **Experimental Design**: Are the controlled experiments well-designed? Is the factorial design adequate? Are confounds properly controlled?\n\n2. **Statistical Rigor**: Are the statistical tests appropriate? Are effect sizes reported alongside p-values? Is multiple comparison correction applied? Are sample sizes adequate?\n\n3. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to reproduce? Are hyperparameters, prompts, sampling procedures described?\n\n4. **Internal Validity**: Could alternative explanations account for the findings? Are the causal claims justified?\n\n5. **Coverage vs Design**: The paper claims \"first controlled empirical study\" — assess whether the experimental coverage (3 tasks, 2 models, 2 embeddings) is sufficient or if gaps weaken the claims.\n\n6. **Metric Design**: Is SCS (cosine similarity of type definition embeddings) a well-motivated confusability measure? Does the BCS aggregation make sense?\n\n7. **Negative Results**: Are the negative results (SDGD failure, LLaMA null, DocRED null) properly interpreted? Could they be artifacts of experimental design?\n\n8. **Specific Weaknesses**: Identify 3-5 concrete methodological weaknesses with specific suggestions for improvement.\n\n## Output Format\n\nProvide your review in this exact structure:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methodology & Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences summarizing the paper's contribution from a methods perspective]\n\n### Strengths\n[Bulleted list, 3-5 items]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Bulleted list, 3-5 items, each with specific evidence and actionable suggestion]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[3-5 specific questions]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Any minor methodological concerns]\n\n### Confidence Score\n[1-5 scale: 1=not confident, 5=very confident]\n\n### Preliminary Rating\n[1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept]\nInclude a one-sentence justification.\n```\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is a paper with many negative/null results — evaluate whether the negative results themselves are informative contributions or just failures.","agentId":"a41904d0543cf3ace","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## R1 Review: Methodology & Experiments\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper investigates whether schema complexity in LLM-based information extraction is one-dimensional or multi-dimensional, using controlled factorial experiments across three IE tasks and two models. The central methodological contribution is the operationalization of \"confusability\" via embedding-space cosine similarity and its separation from type count as an independent complexity axis. The paper reports mostly null or weak results for confusability effects and a negative result for confusability-minimizing decomposition, framing these as informative boundary conditions rather than failures.\n\n---\n\n### Strengths\n\n- **Factorial design is appropriate for the core question.** The 2×2 count × BCS design (exp-006, n=48, 12 per cell) is a principled approach for separating additive from interactive effects. The explicit ANOVA framing with F-statistics and p-values reported for main effects and interaction term is more rigorous than the correlational approaches common in prompt engineering papers.\n\n- **Controlled ablation coverage is unusually thorough.** Five decomposition variants (single-pass, SDGD-zero, SDGD-cal, random batching, per-type) with results on two datasets gives a clear picture of the decomposition failure mode. The inclusion of a random batching baseline (exp-013) is particularly important — without it, the SDGD failure would be uninterpretable.\n\n- **The centrality confound identification (Finding 3) is a genuine methodological contribution.** The ρ=+0.195/+0.212 sign reversal on MAVEN is an observable artifact with a plausible causal mechanism (high-frequency types have more co-occurrence partners, inflating BCS). This is a concrete, reproducible warning for future work using aggregate confusability scores.\n\n- **Embedding proxy validation (exp-015) is the right experiment to run before interpreting all other SCS-based results.** Reporting the 42% false positive rate and the MiniLM ρ=0.098 (NS) result honestly, before using SCS as a predictor throughout the paper, reflects appropriate epistemic caution.\n\n- **The SDGD failure explanation (loss of disambiguation context) is testable and specific**, and the authors correctly note that SDGD-cal ≈ SDGD-zero ≈ random (all NS) makes the null result robust rather than contingent on a single implementation choice.\n\n---\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- **Multiple comparison correction is absent and the selective significance pattern is not adequately flagged.** The paper reports 7 SCS-confusion correlation tests (Finding 1) and highlights 3 as significant. With no Bonferroni or FDR correction, the expected number of false positives at α=0.05 is 0.35, making 3 discoveries unsurprising. The paper treats the 3/7 pattern as evidence of \"model/embedding-dependence\" rather than noise. Correction is essential: if Bonferroni-corrected α=0.007, the r=0.042 (p=0.068) and r=0.098 (p=0.082) results are already NS, and the question becomes whether the remaining significant results survive. *Actionable suggestion*: Apply FDR correction across all pairwise and aggregate SCS tests and restate which findings survive.\n\n- **The 2×2 ANOVA (exp-006) has a serious cell-construction confound.** \"High confusability\" and \"low confusability\" batches are constructed by selecting type subsets from the same dataset. This means cells differ not only in BCS but systematically in which types are included — if high-BCS types are also rarer, more abstract, or harder in other unmeasured ways, the BCS F-statistic is confounded. The paper does not report whether type frequency, instance count, or annotation density are balanced across cells. *Actionable suggestion*: Report type frequency distributions and instance counts per ANOVA cell, or use stratified sampling that explicitly matches these covariates.\n\n- **The SCS metric conflates two distinct constructs without justification.** Cosine similarity between type *definition* embeddings measures semantic proximity in the embedding space of definitions, which is one possible operationalization of confusability. However, a model's tendency to confuse two types depends on how those types appear in *context*, not just definition similarity. The 42% false positive rate (exp-015) is direct evidence this is an imperfect proxy. The paper does not consider alternative operationalizations (e.g., prompt-based discriminability, definition length, co-occurrence frequency in training data) or discuss when definition-embedding SCS is and is not a valid proxy. *Actionable suggestion*: Include at minimum a comparison of Qwen3-embedding SCS vs. a simple lexical overlap baseline between type names/definitions, to anchor the embedding-space metric's value-add.\n\n- **The DocRED null result is explained post-hoc without experimental support.** The paper frames DocRED as a \"boundary condition\" where the proposed mechanisms do not operate, attributing this to relational extraction's context-dependence. This is plausible but not tested: no ablation removes the proposed confound (e.g., testing RE with entity type cues suppressed) nor is there any analysis of *why* count curves are flat for RE. The null result for an entire task type means the paper's findings may not generalize to RE at all, yet the title and abstract claim generality across \"LLM information extraction.\" *Actionable suggestion*: Either narrow the scope claims explicitly to span/mention-level extraction tasks, or add a diagnostic analysis of DocRED errors to test whether the proposed mechanisms are simply absent or masked.\n\n- **Effect sizes are reported inconsistently and are uniformly small, but this is not discussed.** r=0.156 (Finding 1, Qwen3/Few-NERD) explains ~2.4% of variance. The 4.8× \"extreme confusability\" ratio (Finding 2) comes from a selected extreme-vs-random comparison rather than the ANOVA; the ANOVA BCS F=2.93 p=0.094 is marginal and not significant. The miss-rate increase 0.28→0.55 is the strongest effect in the paper, but this is a count effect, not a confusability effect. The confusability effects that survive are consistently small. The paper does not compute power for the ANOVA nor discuss whether the study was adequately powered to detect plausible effect sizes. *Actionable suggestion*: Compute post-hoc power for the 2×2 ANOVA given observed effect sizes, and add an explicit section discussing practical significance vs. statistical significance of confusability effects.\n\n---\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **Cell balance in exp-006**: How were the 12 schema subsets per ANOVA cell selected? Specifically, are type frequency (instance counts per type), type abstractness, and annotation density matched across high-BCS and low-BCS cells? If cells were selected purely on BCS with no covariate matching, the ANOVA results are confounded.\n\n2. **Multiple testing**: Across all reported correlation tests (SCS vs. confusion, BCS vs. miss rate, BCS vs. substitution rate, etc.), how many total hypothesis tests are conducted in the paper? If the authors apply FDR correction, which results survive at q<0.05?\n\n3. **LLaMA-3.3-70B scope**: The cross-model validation (exp-012) uses LLaMA only on Few-NERD with Qwen3 embeddings. Given that LLaMA produces an NS result (r=0.042, p=0.068), is it possible the Qwen3 SCS metric is simply better calibrated to Qwen3's own confusion patterns? Was LLaMA evaluated with LLaMA-space embeddings?\n\n4. **SDGD context hypothesis**: The proposed explanation for SDGD failure — that decomposition removes disambiguation context — predicts that adding explicit cross-type contrast examples to each sub-batch should recover performance. Is this prediction tested anywhere? Without a direct test, the explanation remains a hypothesis rather than a finding.\n\n5. **Prompt and sampling details**: For reproducibility, what is the exact prompt template used for single-pass extraction across the three tasks? Were outputs generated with greedy decoding or sampling? What temperature/top-p settings were used? These details appear absent from the main paper and are essential for replication.\n\n---\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n- Table count (25 tables) is excessive for a 13-page paper; many tables appear to contain results that could be consolidated, making it difficult to identify primary evidence for each finding.\n- The paper has 5 references, which is critically sparse for a \"related work\" section claiming this is the \"first controlled empirical study\" — this claim requires engagement with prior work on schema reduction, type hierarchy, and IE with LLMs.\n- The error taxonomy (45% spurious, 25% missed, 18% substitution, 12% boundary) is reported globally but not broken down by count condition or confusability bin, which would directly connect the taxonomy to Findings 1 and 2.\n- \"Training-free\" in the title is accurate but could be perceived as a limitation rather than a feature; the paper would benefit from one sentence justifying why training-free is the appropriate scope.\n- p<10^-11 and p<10^-42 for n-driven Pearson correlations indicate extremely large sample sizes — the large n makes even trivially small r values significant, which reinforces the concern that effect sizes rather than p-values should be the primary evidence.\n\n---\n\n### Confidence Score\n\n**4 / 5** — The experimental setup is described with enough specificity to evaluate the design, though absence of prompt templates and cell-construction details for the ANOVA introduce residual uncertainty.\n\n---\n\n### Preliminary Rating\n\n**3 / 6 — Weak Reject**\n\nThe paper addresses a genuine and underexplored question with appropriate factorial methodology and commendable transparency about null results, but the absence of multiple-comparison correction across a selective 3/7 significance pattern, unverified covariate balance in the key ANOVA, and a complete null result for one of three tasks without diagnostic follow-up leave the primary positive claims insufficiently supported relative to the claim of a \"controlled empirical study.\""}],"totalDurationMs":51619,"totalTokens":18109,"totalToolUseCount":0,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15727,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":2379,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":15727},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":2379,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15727,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":15727,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}}}
